30988:     "note": "15 KB, having been at 568 bytes and looking exactly like a stall when first sampled -- the carrier's own short-answer warning, confirmed again.  Ratified the splice ('`return 0` is a guest DBE; `break` is not legal here'), the per-site bits against the not-mutually-exclusive warrant, and the `warned`-after-`mte_reg` layout the LINEAR comments assert.  Flagged the catch-all's queue id independently of kimi."
34130:     "note": "PASS 8, PACKET-FED (8.2 KB packet; NO burn this time: 5686 b).  VERDICT NOT COMPLETE: (1) TxCTL bits 5-7 held at init by every row (the ovf rows stop at count 1) -> `&& (reg & 0xe0) == 0' invisible -- TRUE, in closure, rows owed (`ovfhi*'); (2) TxCTL bits 2-3 / TxICR bits 6-7 unlisted -- E by writer census (deepseek's cell); 'bits 0-4 by presets' unevidenced -> bit 3 is in fact never written (kimi's cell, listing corrected); (3) `d->warned' not shown per arm -- covered by the shared block bits (rb2 t2ctl/t2icr) and `ipl noig2'/`pscale noig2'; E at SCCERR; and it is right that the helper's latch read is outside the 'body reads' rule and is carried as its own line; (4) the transfer wording: under M_W4FALL_ALL the GENCTL rb4/rb8 rows do NOT decline -- they are that mutant's killing rows and were MODIFIED (lanes), so 'additions cannot un-kill' does not reach them -- TRUE: the by-value GENCTL kill is UNMEASURED since the lane change -> M_W4FALL_ALL re-measured in part 8; (5) minor: the caught-value set is per arm {3,1,0} with `latch all zero' owed; `&& pending_timer1 == 0' at all twelve is the symmetric unmeasured form (predict pend1 rows red) -- disclosed; a diff count proves divergence, not the site (the driver also prints the first added line); the twelve rows are itemised in the diff.  Arithmetic verified.  A reading."
46545:   "id": "vmedefaultkill",
46550:   "note": "[2026-10-20] IN WORK as round #568 (the loop's singular task).  RE-WITNESSED on HEAD 5dbc000e by an Opus 5.5 sub-agent (a measurement, not a panel seat): 7/7 sites rc 1, the free-running read silent, the free-running T1CMP control hit its breakpoint (regress/artifacts/vmedefaultkill/witness_20261020/SUMMARY.md).  PASS 1 (design) FIRED on the owner's 2026-10-20 roster -- GLM 5.3, grok-4.7, GPT-6 Astra, Fable 5.1 -- plus Argon (Gemini web) as a second packet-fed lens: all five PROCEED WITH CHANGES (regress/artifacts/vmedefaultkill/pass1_20261020/ADJUDICATION_p1.md).  Design kept: exact case labels for the 26 header-named registers (Fable dissented for a bound test in default:, recorded); no `odata = 0`; both announce prints become measurements (64-bit write value, served value); a compile-time latch-capacity check; default: returns 0 with corrected wording.  The detector grows the rows the seats found missing (write-side and mixed-direction announce, non-word stores, one session per fault, interior pins at 0x29/0x2a, a free-running negative control).  ||  [2026-10-09] QUEUE 7n-4 ANSWERED; OWNER: MEASURE FIRST.  Fable 5.1 (regress/artifacts/fablequeue7n_20261009/fable5_rule7n_4.md): the owner's FAULT ruling binds population 1 (accesses the emulator cannot service) and reaches neither other population.  POPULATION 2 -- the in-tree OpenBSD 2.2 guest's MASTERCTL (r/w), MASTER3, MASTER4, MASTER4MOD at attach and IRQSTAT (r) / IRQCLR (w) in the abort handler -- went back to the owner, with the file's own sibling idiom (DECLINE + announce, the #501 shape) as the default pending re-ruling and BACKED ACCEPT not licensed.  GPT-6 Astra CONFIRM WITH CHANGES (7) (regress/artifacts/fablequeue7n_20261009/astra_7n4/codex_astra.txt): six registers, SEVEN access sites (five initialisation operations, straight-line once the VME2 branch runs; IRQSTAT read when the abort handler runs; IRQCLR written only if the abort-status bit is set); vmematch() reads no VME register, so 'already matched (on offset 0, served)' is false; the guest panic under FAULT is CONDITIONAL on unmeasured CMMU/exception state (DPFSR comes from a memory-mapped CMMU read; panictrap() only when pcb_onfault is 0); the five neighbouring cased registers call debug() on every access -- 'latched' is a proposal, not this file's idiom; and serving IRQSTAT as 0 makes an invoked abort handler return before clearing the interrupt or calling nmihand().  OWNER 2026-10-09 (AskUserQuestion): MEASURE FIRST.  MEASURED THE SAME EVENING (agy, rung 3, unmodified -E mvme88k -e mvme187, regress/artifacts/vmedefaultkill/measure_20261009/): one session per site per binary, each with a cased T1CMP round-trip (0x12345678 back in all three binaries), a liveness `or` and an unassemble check.  HEAD: all SEVEN sites end the emulator (rc 1).  FAULT sketch (the arm returns 0): every site vectors to VBR+0x18 with DMT0/DMD0/DMA0 recorded, but the data CMMU's PFSR and PFAR read 0 through a guest `ld` at 0xfff7f108/0xfff7f10c -- the guest's eh.S stores exactly that word as dpfsr, so trap.c sees neither a guarded bus error (0x3) nor a page/segment fault (0x4/0x5) and reaches panictrap() with pcb_onfault 0: the attach PANICS (read by the main loop in trap.c; a modelled bus error would not save it either, since guarded recovery covers only badaddr()'s window).  DECLINE sketch (odata = 0; break): reads return 0, writes are dropped, liveness green -- attach completes.  The abort handler would read IRQSTAT 0 and return as spurious without nmihand(), but nothing in the model raises the VME2 abort interrupt today.  OWNER 2026-10-09, on that measurement: population 2 -> DECLINE + ANNOUNCE ONCE (reads 0, writes dropped, one fatal() per register with the house '(once)' marker, #501's shape); population 1 keeps the FAULT ruling.  Populations 3 and 4 are filed as vmewidthlanes.  ||  FILED 2026-09-10 from the GPT-6 Astra exit audit, listed among the six E1 predicates. VERIFIED LOCALLY.\n\n*** THIS ONE IS WORSE THAN THE USUAL SHAPE AND THE REASON IS THE DIAGNOSTIC LEVEL. ***  Every other unimplemented-register kill in this queue announces with `fatal()', which this fork has measured CANNOT be silenced by -q (debugmsg.c: fatal() has no early-out and goes straight to va_debug).  This one uses **debug()**, which DOES have an early-out -- `if ((quiet_mode && !ss) || v < 0) return;' -- so at default verbosity the message is not printed at all.  **The emulator therefore dies with no output explaining why.**  A user sees the process vanish; a gate sees a non-zero exit and no diagnostic to grep.  That makes this the hardest member of the class to DIAGNOSE, independent of how hard it is to trigger.\n\nREACHABILITY: machine_mvme88k.c installs it -- `vme addr=0x%x', VME2_BASE -- unconditionally on a machine this project actively boots (the same constructor attaches the PCC2 the recent rounds are about).  Probing a bus controller's register space is exactly what firmware does.\n\nDISPOSITION: member 5 (FAULT) is AVAILABLE -- M88K is one of the two families for which memory_rw.c delivers a failed-device exception -- so `return 0' gives the guest the bus error real VME hardware would.  Member 3 (decline + latched announce) is the alternative and would need the announce PROMOTED from debug() to fatal() to be worth anything, which is a second change the round must make deliberately rather than inherit.\n\nNOT MEASURED, no witness; the rung-3 attempt is owed.\n\n*** DESIGN REVIEW 2026-09-10: DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE, and the review RATIFIES PROMOTING THE DIAGNOSTIC -- 'including deliberate promotion of its default-arm diagnostics to fatal()'. ***  So the second change this row flagged is not an optional rider; it is part of the disposition.  FAULT is mechanically available on M88K and is ruled OUT for the same reason as [[crimewidthkill]]: this is a PRESENT device refusing an unimplemented register, not an absent one, and a bus error would assert something about the hardware that nothing establishes.\n\nREACHABILITY CONFIRMED AND MADE PRECISE: the registered aperture is 4 KiB while the exact-offset switch recognises only TEN offsets, and the device's own vendored header names further real registers -- the second timer, interrupt status, master mapping controls -- that have NO cases at all.  So both reads and writes reach the default arm through ordinary register access, not through anything exotic.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 HARVEST (codex GPT-6 Astra at max, 12,795 b, COMPLETE).  I FORECLOSED THE FAULT OPTION BY ASSERTION, AND THIS PROJECT'S OWN QUEUE FILE RECOMMENDS THE OPPOSITE. ***\n\n**THE PROCESS FAILURE FIRST, because it is the finding.**  My brief's settled-premises block said *\"a bus fault was ruled OUT\"* -- a PRESENT device refusing an unimplemented register, not an ABSENT one.  The seat obeyed and said so: *\"the caller behaviour is accepted from your adjudication; `memory_rw.c` was outside this review.\"*  **The option was removed before the panel could argue with it.**  That is the option-set rule's own failure mode -- *a brief that lists two options gets a panel arguing about two options* -- committed by the person who wrote the rule into the brief.\n\nTHREE FACTS CUT THE OTHER WAY, each verified by opening the file:\n  1. `OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:18236-18239`, the row this round is working, says *\"member 5 (FAULT) is **available** ... so `return 0` gives the guest the bus error real VME hardware would.\"*\n  2. `memory_rw.c:595-624` delivers it -- `res == 0` -> `res = -1` -> `m88k_exception(cpu, M88K_EXCEPTION_DATA_ACCESS, 0)`.  **And its comment at `:615-619` is the part that matters: *\"This is enough for OpenBSD/mvme88k's badaddr() implementation.\"*  The guest that runs on this exact machine PROBES WITH BUS FAULTS ON PURPOSE.**\n  3. `machine_mvme88k.c:106, :120, :123` install `dev_unreadable` THREE TIMES in the same MVME187 arm, within ~30 lines of the `vme` attach, and `dev_unreadable.c:41-44` is the whole device: `return 0;`.  **FAULT is that constructor's own idiom for absent hardware.**\n\n*The present/absent distinction may still win -- it is a real argument.  But it must win on the evidence, and it has not been tested against any of the three.*  Re-fired to codex as `codex_faultreopen_20260911` with `memory_rw.c` PERMITTED this time, asking for both sides argued before a ruling.  Also put to the owner.\n\n*** AND THE SHIPPED ANSWER'S OWN SCOPE DISCLOSURE NEVER MADE IT INTO THE ANSWER. ***  The `.err` holds a SECOND, ABANDONED RENDER whose surviving tail (`:1484-1486`) says *\"I did not inspect `memory_rw.c` or independently verify the 'two families' claim\"* -- the sharpest scope statement in the whole exchange, sitting in the file nobody reads.  **Read the `.err` tail, not only the `.txt`.**\n\n=== FINDINGS THAT CHANGE THE EDIT ===\n\n**(A) DELETING `exit(1)` DOES NOT GIVE ZERO READS, AND ONE ALIAS IS NONZERO FROM RESET WITH NO GUEST WRITE AT ALL.**  `dev_vme.c:65-66` loads `odata = d->reg[relative_addr / sizeof(uint32_t)]` **before** the switch, unconditionally on `MEM_READ`; `:161-162` seeds the VBR word to `VME2_SET_VBR0(6)+VME2_SET_VBR1(7)` = **0x67000000** at devinit; `VME2_VBR` is `0x0088`.  **So a byte or halfword read at offset 0x89-0x8b takes `default:` and returns 0x67000000 on a freshly constructed machine.**  Under DECLINE the round must add an explicit `odata = 0;` or consciously keep the alias, and the contract must say which.  **Under FAULT the question dissolves** -- nothing is returned.  *That asymmetry is now part of the disposition question rather than a detail under it.*\n\n**(B) THE KILL IS REACHABLE AT SUB-WORD OFFSETS OF *IMPLEMENTED* REGISTERS.**  The switch compares the RAW BYTE OFFSET while storage indexes by `relative_addr / sizeof(uint32_t)`, so a case does not cover all four bytes of its own backing word.  **A byte access at 0x51 -- inside T1CMP -- falls to `default:` and kills the host today.**  Twelve such byte offsets sit inside the four storing registers.  *This is exactly the shape a detector written from the fix's INTENT will miss.*\n\n**(C) THE CENSUS RESOLVES EXACTLY: 4086 of 4096 byte offsets, 1014 of 1024 word offsets**, and it is SCATTERED, not contiguous -- six runs: `0x004..0x028` (10 words), `0x030..0x048` (7), `0x058..0x05c` (2), `0x064..0x068` (2), `0x070..0x07c` (4), `0x08c..0xffc` (989).  **The very first gap is the word ADJACENT to `case 0:`, the offset the file's own comment says OpenBSD probes.**  *The seat correctly REFUSED to name a number without the header and gave bounds instead; both bounds turned out to be the answer.*\n\n=== FINDINGS THAT CHANGE THE RECORD OR THE ORACLE ===\n\n* **`debug()` is NOT silent during machine CONSTRUCTION.**  `emul_executing` is false (`emul.c:67`) until `emul.c:1044`, so at setup `v = verbose = 0`, the `v < 0` early-out does not fire, and the message PRINTS.  The silence is specific to run-time accesses -- **so a witness must drive the access FROM THE GUEST, or the pre-fix symptom is \"printed, then died\" and the round's headline claim does not reproduce.**\n* **The stdout-flush caveat points the OTHER way for the premise.**  `va_debug()` uses `printf` with no `fflush` -- but the current code calls `exit(1)` immediately and `exit()` flushes stdio, so the pre-fix silence is ENTIRELY the `debug()` early-out with nothing lost to buffering.  That STRENGTHENS the premise.  The caveat lands on the POST-fix code, where no exit forces the flush.\n* **`#261` CANNOT be tripped by promoting to `fatal()`.**  `debugmsg_break_on_error` (`debugmsg.c:102`) is tested at `:159` inside `debugmsg_va()`, reachable only from `debugmsg()`; `fatal()` (`:384-390`) calls `va_debug()` directly and never enters it.  Corroborated by two in-tree comments that already said so (`dev_asc.c:256`, `dev_wdc.c:113-114`).\n* **My \"roughly a dozen\" non-fatal notices was wrong: NINE active across five named offsets** -- GCSRCTL x1, TCR x2, IRQEN x2, IRQL3 x2, IRQL4 x2.  TCTL's is COMMENTED OUT at `:104`.  Twelve textual, nine active.\n* **The file has NO latch of either kind.**  `struct vme_data` (`:52-54`) holds only `reg[]` and `:158` zeroes it, so a new `int` self-initialises.  House form is `dev_wdc.c:75-79, :115-120`.  A function-static would let one instance silence another.\n* **Install site NARROWED: `machine_mvme88k.c:95-96`, inside `case MACHINE_MVME88K_187:` ONLY.**  My \"unconditionally\" was true only within that arm -- MVME188/MVME197 (`:127-135`) are bare `strdup`+TODO and install nothing.  Window `0xfff40000`-`0xfff40fff`.\n\n*** THE VERDICT/BODY CONTRADICTION, and it is this project's own recorded verdict-line hazard: the verdict ratifies (b) while the body's own (b) cell says **\"define the read fallback explicitly\"** and S6 says deleting `exit(1)` alone does not give zero reads.  A reader stopping at the verdict ships an edit the body calls under-specified. ***\n\n**THE HONEST REFUSAL THAT STANDS:** whether a STOCK BOOT actually travels the `default:` arm is NOT established.  The only firmware evidence in the file -- `case 0:`'s *\"Used by OpenBSD/mvme88k when probing\"* -- proves a SUPPORTED probe exists and says nothing about an unmatched offset being touched.  **The round still owes the rung-3 probe attempt.**\n\n*** OWNER RULING 2026-09-11: **FAULT -- `return 0`**.  The disposition I had foreclosed by assertion is the one adopted. ***\n\nThe `default:` arm returns 0, `memory_rw.c:595-624` converts that to `m88k_exception(cpu, M88K_EXCEPTION_DATA_ACCESS, 0)`, and the guest takes the bus error that `badaddr()` probing expects.  **The previously ratified decline-plus-latched-announce is SUPERSEDED**; the promotion of `debug()` to `fatal()` goes with it, because there is no longer an announce to promote.\n\nWHAT THE RULING BUYS, beyond matching the queue file and the `dev_unreadable` idiom used three times in the same machine arm:\n  * **the `odata` aliasing defect DISSOLVES.**  Under DECLINE the round owed an explicit `odata = 0;` because `dev_vme.c:65-66` loads from `d->reg[]` before the switch and `:161-162` seeds the VBR word to `0x67000000`, so a byte read at 0x89-0x8b returned that value from reset with no guest write.  **Under FAULT nothing is returned at all** and the cell cannot leak.\n  * the announce, the latch, the per-instance-vs-function-static question, and the `#261` interaction all become moot.  *Three of the seven questions I put to the panel were about machinery this disposition does not need.*\n\nWHAT IT STILL OWES:\n  1. **The twelve sub-word offsets inside IMPLEMENTED registers.**  The switch compares the raw byte offset while storage indexes by word, so a byte access at 0x51 -- inside T1CMP -- reaches `default:`.  **Under FAULT that now raises a bus error on a register that DOES exist**, which is a different and arguably worse wrong answer than the host kill it replaces.  *This is the one place the ruling makes something harder rather than easier, and it must be decided explicitly rather than inherited.*\n  2. The rung-3 probe attempt -- whether a stock boot travels the arm is still NOT established, and the seat's refusal on that point stands.\n  3. The detector's oracle changes shape entirely: a fault is observed as an EXCEPTION, not as a returned value or a logged announce, so no row from the decline design survives unchanged.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 -- THE REOPENED PANEL AGREES WITH THE OWNER.  **THE SEAT RULED FAULT TOO**, with `memory_rw.c` PERMITTED this time, and it sharpened one argument while honestly refusing another. ***\n\n*VERDICT: \"FAULT (`return 0`) for the unsupported-access fallback; handle register byte lanes separately.\"*  13,858 b, liveness token returned, ends in a finished sentence, sentinel cleared cleanly.\n\n**ALL THREE OF MY NEW FACTS SURVIVED, and one came back STRONGER than I put it.**  I argued that `dev_unreadable` being installed three times in the MVME187 arm shows FAULT is the constructor's idiom for ABSENT hardware.  The seat found the sharper version: **the board description at `machine_mvme88k.c:35` lists Ethernet hardware, and the constructor substitutes `unreadable` for that Ethernet, marked TODO** (`:106`, with `:120` \"VMES (?): TODO\" and `:123` \"VME devices: TODO\").  **So this constructor already uses faults for hardware OMITTED FROM EMULATION, not merely for hardware that is absent from the board.**  That is precisely the case at hand, and it weakens my original categorical present/absent argument further than I had.\n\n**AND IT REFUSED THE HARDWARE CLAIM, which is the right refusal:** *\"It does not establish that real VME hardware would fault at every offset reaching this `default:`.  That hardware claim remains unproved.\"*  Registering a `0x1000` region establishes **the emulator's dispatch aperture, not that physical hardware acknowledges every transaction inside it** -- and the header specifies no legal widths, no reserved-offset responses and no timeout behaviour.  *The disposition is therefore justified as \"an access the emulator cannot service\", NOT as \"this is what the hardware does\".  The round's record must say it that way.*\n\n*** AND IT REFUTED THE PRESENT/ABSENT FRAMING ON ITS OWN TERMS, not just its weight: \"the device is present and the register is not\" DOES NOT DESCRIBE THE WHOLE DEFAULT ARM. ***  The header names `VME2_DMACTL`, `VME2_T2CMP`, `VME2_T2COUNT` and `VME2_PRESCALE` -- **registers whose exact offsets have NO switch case.**  Those are named register definitions lacking emulator handling, not addresses the source identifies as nonexistent.  So the `default:` arm is a MIXTURE of three populations, and I had been reasoning about it as one:\n  1. offsets with no register defined anywhere -- FAULT is honest;\n  2. **named registers the emulator simply does not implement** -- FAULT says \"nothing here\" about something the board documents as present;\n  3. sub-word lanes of registers that ARE implemented -- FAULT is plainly wrong.\n\n**THE SEAT'S OWN CAVEAT CONVERGES WITH THE ONE I FLAGGED TO THE OWNER, reached independently:** *\"Interior offsets need their own access-width decision and corresponding oracle; successful removal of the exit does not close that issue.\"*  **Two parties, two routes, same residual.**\n\nWHAT THE ROUND NOW OWES, consolidated:\n  * a decision on population 3 (the twelve sub-word lanes) that is NOT inherited from the population-1 ruling;\n  * a stated position on population 2 -- whether an unimplemented-but-documented register should fault or be backed;\n  * the rung-3 probe attempt, still outstanding;\n  * *\"probe recovery and an ordinary driver's fault response require integration validation\"* -- the review establishes the dispatch mechanism and **explicitly does not establish continued bootability**.\n\n*The seat also declined to verify the queue-file recommendation, because `OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md` was outside its permitted set, and said so rather than treating my quotation as a checked repository finding.  That is the honest form and is recorded as an answer, not a gap.*",
46576:     "ev": "regress/artifacts/vmedefaultkill/measure_20261009/agy_vme1009.txt; regress/artifacts/vmedefaultkill/measure_20261009/run_HEAD.out; regress/artifacts/vmedefaultkill/measure_20261009/run_FAULT.out; regress/artifacts/vmedefaultkill/measure_20261009/run_DECLINE.out",
46581:     "ev": "regress/artifacts/vmedefaultkill/pass1_20261020/codex_vmep1.txt",
